        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync field guide</div>
        <h2>What is HubSpot CRM architecture?</h2>
        <p class="guide-answer">HubSpot CRM architecture is the design behind a portal: the objects that hold your records, how they relate, the pipelines and stages that describe your process, the lifecycle definitions, and the permissions that control who changes what. It is the operating model of your revenue team, written into HubSpot.</p>
        <p class="intro">This blueprint gives you a practical order for designing HubSpot so teams trust the data, managers trust the forecast, and future changes do not turn into another cleanup project. It is how we start every <a href="/solutions/hubspot-implementation/">HubSpot implementation</a>.</p>

        <section id="motion">
          <h2>Where should HubSpot architecture start?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Start with the revenue motion, not the settings. Map how a person moves from first touch to customer and expansion, and write down the questions the business needs the CRM to answer. Separating the questions from the tools stops the portal becoming a copy of every old process.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Document the lifecycle from first touch through customer and advocate.</li>
            <li>List the decisions leadership makes from CRM data each month.</li>
            <li>Write the rules that move a record from one stage to the next.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="lifecycle-example">
          <h2>What does a worked lifecycle look like?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">A worked lifecycle names each stage, the event that moves a record into it, and who owns the record there. Writing it as a table makes gaps obvious, such as a stage nobody owns or a handoff with no trigger, before anything is configured.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Entry event</th><th>Owner</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Lead</td><td>Form submitted or imported with consent</td><td>Marketing</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Marketing qualified lead</td><td>Agreed fit and engagement criteria met</td><td>Marketing</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Sales qualified lead</td><td>Sales accepts the lead</td><td>Sales</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Opportunity</td><td>Deal created and associated</td><td>Sales</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Customer</td><td>Deal closed won</td><td>Account management</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          </div>
          <p>The definitions behind each stage are covered in <a href="/resources/hubspot-lifecycle-stages-explained/">HubSpot lifecycle stages explained</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="objects">
          <h2>How should objects, pipelines and stages fit together?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Objects describe what you manage, pipelines describe the process, and stages describe progress through it. Keep the layers separate. A deal stage should be a verifiable buyer milestone, such as "proposal accepted", not an internal task like "send proposal", so the pipeline reports reality.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>One source of truth for each business concept.</li>
            <li>Short, observable stage names, each with an exit criterion.</li>
            <li>Custom objects only for things with their own records and lifecycle (an Enterprise feature; see <a href="/resources/hubspot-professional-vs-enterprise/">Professional vs Enterprise</a>).</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="pipelines">
          <h2>When should you add a new pipeline?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Add a pipeline only when the process, ownership or reporting is genuinely different, such as new business versus renewals. Teams that follow the same process should share one pipeline and be told apart with properties or teams; extra pipelines split reporting and multiply automation.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="catalogue">
          <h2>What belongs in the property catalogue?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">A property catalogue lists every custom property with its internal name, label, object, data type, allowed values, a one-line definition, owner and whether it is required. Before anyone creates a property, they check the catalogue; that single habit prevents most duplicate fields.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Internal name</td><td>lead_source_detail</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Object and type</td><td>Contact, dropdown</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Allowed values</td><td>The agreed list, no free text</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Definition</td><td>Where this lead first came from, at campaign level</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Owner</td><td>Marketing operations</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Required</td><td>Yes, on all forms and imports</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="permissions">
          <h2>How should permissions and governance be built in?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Build them in from the start. Create permission sets by role, decide who can edit sensitive properties, limit exports and super admins, and publish naming conventions before teams create their own fields and workflows. A scalable portal makes the safe path the easy path.</p>
